WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.160
A lot of the discussion around commander&nbsp;
deckbuilding often revolves around the commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.440
and this makes sense—it’s right there&nbsp;
in the name of the format. In specific,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.000
how to best utilize your command&nbsp;
zone is a big area of discussion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.080
and certainly one that I’ve dabbled in plenty.&nbsp;
It’s good when you can get more out of a card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.360
in your deck that you’ll always have access to.&nbsp;
However, there’s a specific deckbuilding choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:27.840
I’ve noticed that results from this goal, and&nbsp;
it’s one that kind of bothers me. That choice is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.280
when a player chooses to take their lovely synergy&nbsp;
engine commander and turn it into a combo piece.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.240
Before I go any further, I should make it&nbsp;
clear what I’m not talking about. I’m not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.520
talking about a player building a combo deck where&nbsp;
the commander is one of the combo pieces. No, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.120
that combo is the deck’s primary gameplan, that’s&nbsp;
actually a great way to build a deck—utilizing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.880
commander in a combo generally makes that combo&nbsp;
more compact, which gives additional deck space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.520
for interaction, support cards, and alternative&nbsp;
gameplans. My two best decks involve the commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.200
being used in a combo for this exact reason, and&nbsp;
I’d recommend that to anybody who’s looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.240
a powerful starting point for a combo deck.&nbsp;
What I’m talking about here is a deck where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.600
the primary gameplan is something other than&nbsp;
a combo that is focused around the commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.720
and a combo that involves the commander is&nbsp;
tossed in as an addition to that. On the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.880
this doesn’t seem like a bad idea—you’ve&nbsp;
already got part of the combo right there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.280
and having that combo might be decent as an&nbsp;
alternate wincon. This logic works so long as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.080
the combo isn’t significantly better than the&nbsp;
primary gameplan, and especially if the combo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:39.080
pieces also fit into that plan. If I’m building a&nbsp;
Samut beatdown deck, Combat Celebrant and Sword of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.720
Hearth and Home are both great cards for the deck&nbsp;
in general, and the fact that they also combo with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.240
the haste the commander provides is just an added&nbsp;
bonus that can allow for explosive wins on certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:54.880
boardstates. Where things become a problem is when&nbsp;
the combo in question is one that is out of place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.840
and far more powerful than the deck’s normal&nbsp;
gameplan, and this is for a couple reasons.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.080
The first is one I’ve talked about and alluded&nbsp;
to before, and this is the lesser of the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.520
issues in my view—the vibes issue. If you build&nbsp;
a deck that’s mainly doing one thing but has some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:15.200
other much more powerful thing it can draw&nbsp;
into 10-20% of the time, that’s gonna lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.880
to some table whiplash. People come into games&nbsp;
with expectations of how their opponents’ decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:25.240
are going to function, and this is fundamental&nbsp;
to any deckbuilding game that requires skill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:30.120
Those expectations rely on decks following some&nbsp;
sort of logic, and when a deck does a mixture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.040
of relatively weak things and wildly powerful&nbsp;
things with no rhyme or reason, it’s much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.440
difficult for people playing against that deck&nbsp;
to conceptualize it and strategize around it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.760
If you’re looking for a luck-based game&nbsp;
with fantasy aesthetics this might be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:47.720
workable situation for you, but on some level it&nbsp;
represents a breakdown of how multiplayer strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.800
games work which a lot of players might not find&nbsp;
enjoyable. This reason actually applies to any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.600
situation where stronger and weaker plays coexist,&nbsp;
regardless of whether the commander is involved.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.520
The other issue with making your commander&nbsp;
a combo piece is the much more tangible one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:07.160
and that’s that what you do with your commander&nbsp;
will inform how your opponents react to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:13.320
commander. When I’m playing against a Zaxara deck&nbsp;
and I have a spare moment to cast a removal spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:18.080
the commander is generally going to be a&nbsp;
medium-value removal target. Certainly, the ramp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.960
it gives is useful, and the X spell ability can&nbsp;
be quite good if the Zaxara player has the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.720
set up to utilize it, but there will still be lots&nbsp;
of times where I’m mostly comfortable with Zaxara&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:34.480
sticking around for a bit. However, according to&nbsp;
EDHRec, 46% of Zaxara decks run Freed from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:40.280
Real, a card that can produce infinite mana with&nbsp;
Zaxara’s ramp ability. And that’s infinite colored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:46.960
mana in an X-spells deck, so the game will often&nbsp;
end right then and there when that happens. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:53.040
I know my opponent is among those 46% and I don’t&nbsp;
wanna hold up mana to remove it later, that Zaxara&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:58.440
immediately becomes a top priority for removal.&nbsp;
If I remove it and they had Freed from the Real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:05.200
in hand, that’s just good defensive play, but the&nbsp;
other 85% of the time the player probably just had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.400
some hydras and ramp in hand and has experienced&nbsp;
an entirely unnecessary setback in their goofy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.480
midrange deck just because they decided to make&nbsp;
their commander part of an infinite combo. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.600
sucks for everybody involved—the hydra player gets&nbsp;
their totally fair-magic gameplan slowed down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.080
and a different player might get to keep&nbsp;
a threatening value engine and pull ahead.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:30.360
As another example, let’s talk about Tivit, Seller&nbsp;
of Secrets. Now, Tivit is a bit different from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.800
Zaxara in that its combo plan is often the&nbsp;
primary plan. While I’d estimate the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:41.000
of Real-Freed Zaxara players who would consider&nbsp;
their deck a genuine combo deck is pretty low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.240
Tivit’s one card infinite combo with Time Sieve&nbsp;
is the first thing a lot of people think of when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:51.760
they think about the commander. And indeed, we see&nbsp;
72% of players are running this combo. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.440
if we dig a bit deeper, we can also see that&nbsp;
about 45% of players are playing a Tivit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:01.160
deck that is based around voting. Sorting&nbsp;
by inclusion of Brago’s Representative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.080
we can see that people building this sort of&nbsp;
deck are running lots of voting-oriented cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.560
as well some flicker and artifact synergies—seems&nbsp;
like a pretty reasonable mid-power deck. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.080
say it’s turn 5 or 6 and this sort of&nbsp;
Tivit player is casting their commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:20.880
and let’s also say I’m holding up a counterspell.&nbsp;
Well, Tivit is certainly a bit concerning—the card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.080
is a strong engine, and him sticking around to&nbsp;
get multiple triggers could lead to the Tivit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.640
player pulling ahead. However, there are plenty&nbsp;
of cases where I’d be fine with Tivit resolving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:34.840
especially if there are more opposing turns before&nbsp;
mine. What if another opponent is pulling ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.160
and I want a chance at nailing a critical spell&nbsp;
of theirs? What if I have a big board I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.480
protect from potential board wipes? What if I’m&nbsp;
holding up a board wipe and have the ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:48.320
to clear out the Tivit later with counterspell&nbsp;
backup anyway? In those cases, I might just let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:54.920
the player have their Tivit and get a bit of mana&nbsp;
and card advantage. But wait, I forgot to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.920 --> 00:06:02.040
63% of voting-oriented Tivit decks run Time Sieve.&nbsp;
Statistically speaking, no shot that’s resolving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:06.680
get your one-card infinite combo deck out of&nbsp;
here, nerd. Once again, this Tivit player could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:12.520
just be looking to do fun voting things and have&nbsp;
a grand experiment in mildly-rigged democracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.640
but unfortunately for them, they decided that&nbsp;
grand experiment in democracy also needed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:23.080
one-card infinite combo. So, no, I think&nbsp;
I’ll pass on that one, thank you very much.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.800
This principle is most pronounced with infinite&nbsp;
combos, but it also applies to other scenarios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:33.240
where a card radically changes the nature of&nbsp;
what a commander does. Take Atraxa—you can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.440
all sorts of things with the commander, pumping up&nbsp;
+1/+1 counters, loyalty counters, charge counters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:43.440
storage counters, and more, and all of these&nbsp;
can work alongside each other just fine. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.360
all except for one. The second you throw&nbsp;
some poison counters on your opponents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:53.240
the commander immediately reads “each turn I get&nbsp;
a poison counter” for all of your opponents, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:58.120
their removal choices will reflect that. Oh, you&nbsp;
were hoping to do some funny superfriends things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:03.920
with Atraxa now? No you’re not. Even worse, since&nbsp;
poison counters are almost impossible to remove,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:09.400
there’s a certain terrifying inevitably that’s&nbsp;
present. Even if Atraxa isn’t in play currently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:13.400
the fact that she could be in the future will&nbsp;
likely serve as enough of a threat to warrant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:19.280
some very focused rounds of attacks. To fully&nbsp;
put on my social theorizing hat, a big part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.280
casual EDH is figuring out ways to win that your&nbsp;
opponents will allow you to perform the setup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:29.280
for. Examples of this might consist of getting a&nbsp;
large land count before casting a massive spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.440
getting a board of tokens before playing an&nbsp;
overrun effect, setting up some synergy pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.360
before landing a centerpiece card that will allow&nbsp;
the deck to surge out of control, and so on. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:45.320
contrast, poison counter proliferation is about as&nbsp;
visible and visceral as setup gets—your opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:50.840
will be kicking and screaming against the lurching&nbsp;
inevitability of your gameplan, even when it’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:56.560
stronger on paper than doing a good old fashioned&nbsp;
tokens + overrun. That’s not to say that poison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.560
counters are never a valid win plan, but you&nbsp;
need to know what you’re getting yourself into.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:05.920
At the end of the day, that’s the main thing&nbsp;
that all of this really comes down to—knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:10.800
what kind of Magic you want to be playing. If your&nbsp;
commander is serving one purpose for your deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.760
stretching it to fill more roles can be a great&nbsp;
way to up your overall efficiency and consistency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:19.920
but you do need to keep an eye on what&nbsp;
exactly those roles are and whether they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.320
pose dramatically different threats to your&nbsp;
opponents. In my Radha deck, the commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:29.520
serves two primary roles—being a mana dork,&nbsp;
and enabling a couple of powerful lieutenant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.160
creatures. This works well because neither of&nbsp;
these purposes step on each other’s toes—removing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:39.200
the commander early to cut me on mana isn’t a&nbsp;
setback to the lieutenant creatures because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.960
haven’t played any yet, and removing the commander&nbsp;
as a lieutenant enabler isn’t a major setback to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:49.280
mana generation because having Radha as a mana&nbsp;
dork isn’t that important past turn 4 anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:53.120
It also is possible to have your commander&nbsp;
perform roles that conflict in some way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.040
and having that work out just comes back to,&nbsp;
once again, knowing what kind of Magic you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:04.440
to play. In my strongest deck, Malcolm is both&nbsp;
a combo piece and a mana engine. This frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.640
renders him the scariest creature on board,&nbsp;
and also means that him eating a removal spell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:13.440
is a pretty major setback. I account for this by&nbsp;
running a massive pile of counterspells that I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:18.960
cast using the treasures Malcolm generates—I know&nbsp;
exactly how my opponents are going to be feeling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.920
and I’m prepared to say no if they send their&nbsp;
removal spells in the most logical direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.640
I also made the deck as consistent as possible at&nbsp;
getting Time Sieve out, because my opponents are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:32.960
going to be bracing for it anyway in how they&nbsp;
treat Malcolm, so I may as well make sure the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.880
effort I spend defending Malcolm isn’t in service&nbsp;
of janky pirate beatdown. When it comes down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:43.920
it, I’m not trying to give any hard and fast&nbsp;
rules here, I’m just trying to make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:49.800
players have their expectations well-calibrated.&nbsp;
If you’re prepared to fight ruthlessly to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.080
your commander in play, that’s great and I&nbsp;
wish you all the luck in the world in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.440
battle. On the contrary, if you’re just here for&nbsp;
a good time and don’t want to fight that battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:05.560
don’t build your deck in a way that gives&nbsp;
other people extra reasons to kill your stuff.

